            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="proof">
    <p class="eyebrow">Get evidence</p>
    <h2>What proof of work looks like in operations</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need a management title to start building proof that you can operate like a manager. You need one real process to improve and document.
    </p>

    <div class="skills-grid">
      {proofCards.map((card) => (
        <article class="skill-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{card.label}</p>
          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
      <p>
        A simple rule helps here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pick one process you actually touch, document the before, the change, and the after, and put a number on the result.
      </p>
      <p>
        That single asset, explained clearly in an interview, beats a resume full of "coordinated" and "assisted" with nothing measured behind it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="next-step">
    <p class="eyebrow">Action plan</p>
    <h2>What to do next if you are serious about becoming an operations manager</h2>
    <p>
      First, decide which entry route actually fits your situation: climb from an executive or analyst role if you want to start earning now, or invest in a supply chain, BBA, or MBA specialisation if you can afford the time and want a faster route to a management-track title.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, pick a sector deliberately. E-commerce, logistics, and IT services offer the strongest current demand and pay; manufacturing and hospitality offer steadier, more structured ladders at a lower ceiling.
    </p>
    <p>
      Third, ask for ownership of one real KPI as early as you reasonably can, and document the before-and-after story once you move it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fourth, build comfort with at least a basic data tool — Excel at minimum, an ERP dashboard or Power BI where the role uses one — since data-driven decision making now shows up in nearly every promotion conversation in this field.
    </p>
    <p>
      Done deliberately, this path builds a genuine high-income skill portfolio, KPI ownership, cross-functional judgment, and documented proof of process improvement, that keeps paying off well past the first management title, unlike skills tied to one tool or platform. If the decision pressure around this choice is real, explore the wider decision tools on the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/career-skills-compass" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> or talk through your specific situation with <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a> to see how this route weighs against your own background and finances.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <p class="eyebrow">FAQs</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions about becoming an operations manager in India</h2>
